    "q-95699": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95699",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a fair spinner with five regions, red, green, yellow, orange, and blue, lands on yellow in $8$ out of $15$ spins.\n\nII. The probability that a soccer player misses a penalty if the probability of scoring the penalty is $0.48.$\n\nIII. The probability that an archer hits the bull's eye more than $10$ times out of $25$ attempts if the probability of hitting the bull's eye on a single shot is $0.56.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=15$ and $p=\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=1$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =1\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =0.48 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.56.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.56 \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.56) \\\\  & =11 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
