    "q-132247": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132247",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a fair coin lands on tails $5$ times out of $8$ coin flips.\n\nII. The probability of choosing $5$ men in a neighborhood with $40$ adults if the probability that a randomly selected adult is a man is $0.55.$\n\nIII. The probability that a golf player makes less than $3$ putts in $15$ shots at the same hole if the probability of making a putt in each shot is $0.3.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=8$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =8\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=40$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =40\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =22 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40\\cdot (1-0.55) \\\\  & =18 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=15$ and $p=0.3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =4.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
