    "q-132266": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132266",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that more than $3$ flights out of $5$ take off on time from an airport if the probability of a single flight taking off on time is $0.8$ for each flight.\n\nII. The probability of getting $7$ ones on $10$ rolls of a $4$-sided die.\n\nIII. The probability of getting $32$ odd numbers on $80$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=5$ and $p=0.8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =5\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=10$ and $p=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=80$ and $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =80\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =80\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
